# exchange.py -- cricmatch247 multi-ID exchange bet bot.
#
# Same script, same protocol as the khelstake24 and starexch555 instances:
# cricmatch247 runs the identical Laravel exchange white-label, so this is an
# env file, not a fork. Checked live read-only 2026-11-02 rather than assumed
# from the family resemblance -- routes, all three sport pages, the parsers,
# the odds provider, the 400-char limit blob, the sixteen betslip fields and
# the stake/odds rules all matched unchanged, and a real account drove login
# -> balance -> series -> matches -> event page -> live odds -> unmatched list
# through the bot's own code. No bet was placed; see the gate at the bottom.
#
# Copy to .env.cricmatch.exchange (gitignored) and fill in. Run:
#   .venv/bin/python exchange.py --env .env.cricmatch.exchange
#
# This is a SEPARATE PROCESS from the khelstake and starexch instances: its
# own bot token and its own ADMINS_FILE. Two processes must never share an
# admins file -- it is read at import and rewritten wholesale, so they would
# clobber each other.

# Telegram bot token from @BotFather -- a NEW bot, not the khelstake or
# starexch one.
EXCHANGE_BOT_TOKEN=123456:ABC-your-token-here

# Comma/space-separated Telegram user IDs that may run the bot. Not
# changeable from inside the bot, only here (+ restart).
MASTER_ADMIN_ID=6936428138

# The site.
EXCHANGE_SITE_URL=https://cricmatch247.com
# Shown in /start; defaults to the hostname if unset.
EXCHANGE_SITE_NAME=cricmatch247

# ── Accounts sheet ──
# Row 1 = header, row 2+ = USERNAME | PASSWORD. The bot writes each ID's
# balance into column C, so GIVE IT ITS OWN TAB -- a tab another script
# already writes (balance_checker.py writes column C too) would have the two
# processes overwrite each other. Make a tab with USERNAME | PASSWORD |
# BALANCE and name it below.
EXCHANGE_SHEET_ID=your-spreadsheet-id-here
EXCHANGE_WORKSHEET_NAME=EXCHANGE IDS
# Or the gid straight out of the sheet URL (…/edit?gid=1162463240).
# Set, it wins over the name and survives the tab being renamed.
# EXCHANGE_WORKSHEET_GID=
# Service account JSON (gitignored); share the sheet with its client_email
# as Editor -- the bot writes balances back.
SHEET_CREDENTIALS_FILE=service_account.json

# ── Proxies: the part that is genuinely different on this site ──
# One proxy per line, host:port or host:port:user:pass (gitignored list).
# Accounts are handed proxies round-robin at login.
#
# ⚠️ cricmatch247 is the one exchange site here that is fussy about the exit
# IP, and in two separate ways (both from CLAUDE.md section 1, confirmed on
# the casino side of the same domain):
#
#   1. Its AWS ALB/WAF answers a bare 403 for IPs it has flagged as proxies.
#      A WAF-blocked proxy looks EXACTLY like a broken one until you check:
#      ip-api.com/json/<ip>?fields=proxy,hosting. Prefer residential.
#      (The DataImpulse gateway in proxies.txt was served normally on a
#      read-only check 2026-11-02 -- hosting:true but proxy:false -- so it
#      works today; it rotates its exit per connection, so that is luck
#      holding rather than a guarantee.)
#   2. Its edge rate-blocks POST /login on VOLUME, not concurrency: roughly
#      20 logins within a few minutes from one IP trips it, and it then stays
#      blocked ~20 minutes regardless of pacing. A single residential IP
#      clears about 2 logins/min. The plain GET keeps working throughout, so
#      the site looks healthy while every login fails.
#
# The bot names this when it happens -- a login refused at the edge is
# reported as "edge blocked: …", NOT as a wrong password -- so read the
# message before changing credentials.
EXCHANGE_PROXY_FILE=proxies.txt

# Parallel workers. This number is used for BOTH bet placement and the
# login sweep at startup and on /relogin, and the login sweep is the one
# that hurts here: the whole roster logs in this many at a time with no
# pacing. On a single IP keep it at 2; the real fix for a bigger roster is
# more proxy IPs (one per ID, round-robin), not a bigger number -- the block
# is per exit IP and counts logins, so lanes on one IP only reach it sooner.
EXCHANGE_BET_CONCURRENCY=2

# ── Site rules ──
# Read out of cricmatch247's own betslip JS 2026-11-02: it refuses
# stake < 2000 and odds < 1.01 || odds > 5 client-side, with Over/Under
# fancy lines exempt from the odds rule (we never bet those). Same numbers
# as khelstake and starexch.
#
# EXCHANGE_MIN_STAKE=0 means "no client-side minimum -- let the server
# decide". The betslip JS says 2000, but smaller stakes are accepted in
# practice on this platform (operator, 2026-10-22), so copying that number
# here only blocked bets the site would have taken. The server is the
# authority either way: it answers "Min Stake is N" when it refuses one.
# Set a number here only to stop a fat-finger stake before it is sent.
EXCHANGE_MIN_STAKE=0
EXCHANGE_MIN_ODDS=1.01
EXCHANGE_MAX_ODDS=5.0

# Live odds provider. cricmatch247 uses the same host as khelstake and
# starexch (read off its own getMarketBook call), so this stays as the
# default.
# EXCHANGE_ODDS_HOST=https://odds4.starlingpay.co.uk

# Persisted admin list written by /addadmin and /removeadmin. MUST differ
# from the khelstake and starexch instances' (matches the admins.*.json
# gitignore rule).
ADMINS_FILE=admins.exchange.cricmatch.json

# ── Before any multi-ID bet ──
# placeBet and cancelbet are the only two steps not yet exercised on this
# site. Fund one ID and run the confirm screen's 🧪 Test 1 ID: an unmatched
# BACK @1.01 on one account, cancelled immediately. Nothing can match at
# 1.01 (that would need a lay priced at or below it), so it proves
# place -> list -> cancel with zero exposure. Same gate khelstake, starexch
# and playinmatch each went through.
